00:04:41.430 --> 00:04:43.040
system.

00:04:43.040 --> 00:04:46.126
This session is going
to have three parts.

00:04:46.126 --> 00:04:47.500
In the first part
of the session,

00:04:47.500 --> 00:04:49.460
I'm going to talk
about our journey

00:04:49.460 --> 00:04:53.525
for moving our event delivery
system into the cloud.

00:04:53.525 --> 00:04:55.980
In the second part,
my colleague Neville

00:04:55.980 --> 00:04:58.510
will talk more about
the tooling that we've

00:04:58.510 --> 00:05:02.920
been using to analyze the
data once it's delivered.

00:05:02.920 --> 00:05:06.160
Finally, I will get
back on the stage

00:05:06.160 --> 00:05:08.120
to talk more about
the learnings that we

00:05:08.120 --> 00:05:10.960
gathered while rebuilding
our event delivery system.

00:05:15.040 --> 00:05:20.100
Spotify have had a phenomenal
user growth in the past years.

00:05:20.100 --> 00:05:23.270
Only in the last year, we
almost tripled our user base,

00:05:23.270 --> 00:05:28.270
reaching 75 million
monthly active users.

00:05:28.270 --> 00:05:31.580
With the user growth
came the data growth.

00:05:31.580 --> 00:05:36.520
Today, we are shipping around
60 billion events every day

00:05:36.520 --> 00:05:37.563
to our Hadoop cluster.

00:05:41.140 --> 00:05:43.600
Oh, OK.

00:05:43.600 --> 00:05:46.670
All the events are being passed
through our current event

00:05:46.670 --> 00:05:52.930
delivery system, which
is based on Kafka 0.7.

00:05:52.930 --> 00:05:55.280
All the events that
are being delivered

00:05:55.280 --> 00:05:59.200
are being generated on
our clients as a response

00:05:59.200 --> 00:06:01.250
to certain user actions.

00:06:01.250 --> 00:06:05.370
So for example, when
user creates a playlist,

00:06:05.370 --> 00:06:07.495
subscribes to a
playlist, or when

00:06:07.495 --> 00:06:12.040
it finishes listening to a
song, an event is generated.

00:06:12.040 --> 00:06:17.380
This event is delivered
to our Hadoop cluster.

00:06:17.380 --> 00:06:22.600
This system worked fine
until a certain scale.

00:06:22.600 --> 00:06:28.170
But with the load
came the outages.

00:06:28.170 --> 00:06:30.220
Well, by observing
how the system worked,

00:06:30.220 --> 00:06:32.410
we observed a lot
of problems with it.

00:06:32.410 --> 00:06:38.070
Most notable problem was that
this system is way too complex.

00:06:38.070 --> 00:06:41.070
It has way too many components
that one event delivery

00:06:41.070 --> 00:06:43.820
system needs to have,
and on top of that,

00:06:43.820 --> 00:06:48.520
most of those components
are having limited coverage,

00:06:48.520 --> 00:06:51.850
and they're
operationally mature.

00:06:51.850 --> 00:06:57.340
Other problem is that our event
delivery system is stateless.

00:06:57.340 --> 00:06:58.920
That means that
the state is only

00:06:58.920 --> 00:07:01.280
kept on the service
machines themselves

00:07:01.280 --> 00:07:05.060
and on the Hadoop cluster.

00:07:05.060 --> 00:07:08.110
If the Hadoop cluster goes
down, our event delivery

00:07:08.110 --> 00:07:10.960
stops functioning, which
is an operational burden

00:07:10.960 --> 00:07:12.990
for the event delivery
team, because we are not

00:07:12.990 --> 00:07:17.610
responsible for the uptime
of the Hadoop cluster.

00:07:17.610 --> 00:07:22.270
When you combine those
problems with the data growth

00:07:22.270 --> 00:07:26.430
that we had, you get
a recipe for disaster.

00:07:26.430 --> 00:07:32.890
So at this exact point here, we
figured out the system cannot

00:07:32.890 --> 00:07:34.820
keep up with the growth.

00:07:34.820 --> 00:07:36.840
At that point, we start
filtering the events,

00:07:36.840 --> 00:07:38.460
and this is the
drop you can see.

00:07:41.200 --> 00:07:43.880
Considering the shortcomings,
design shortcomings

00:07:43.880 --> 00:07:47.500
of the system that we
observed, the only way forward

00:07:47.500 --> 00:07:53.670
to keep up with the Spotify is a
complete rebuild of the system.

00:07:53.670 --> 00:08:00.430
When we started thinking about
new event delivery system,

00:08:00.430 --> 00:08:02.890
we knew that the new event
delivery system still

00:08:02.890 --> 00:08:06.370
needs to keep the same function
as the current one, that

00:08:06.370 --> 00:08:09.240
meaning that all the
events from the clients

00:08:09.240 --> 00:08:12.390
still need to be delivered
to a Hadoop cluster.

00:08:15.570 --> 00:08:21.300
To have an easier migration,
we wanted to keep the same API.

00:08:21.300 --> 00:08:23.480
That means that all
events are still

00:08:23.480 --> 00:08:26.340
persisted to log via
syslog, and then delivered

00:08:26.340 --> 00:08:28.505
to Hadoop in hourly buckets.

00:08:31.390 --> 00:08:34.419
We also wanted that our
event delivery system

00:08:34.419 --> 00:08:36.909
have a persistent
state implemented

00:08:36.909 --> 00:08:39.950
as a reliable persistent
queue component.

00:08:39.950 --> 00:08:43.350
That way, if the
Hadoop goes down,

00:08:43.350 --> 00:08:49.240
we can just keep on functioning
without any noticeable issues.

00:08:49.240 --> 00:08:55.740
Finally, we wanted to keep the
system as simple as possible.

00:08:55.740 --> 00:09:00.120
Simpler the system gets,
it's easier to operate it

00:09:00.120 --> 00:09:02.200
and to scale it.

00:09:02.200 --> 00:09:05.230
Simple as that.

00:09:05.230 --> 00:09:09.550
We had the basics laid down,
and the only thing left to do

00:09:09.550 --> 00:09:10.845
was to get our hands dirty.

00:09:14.460 --> 00:09:17.300
First, we wanted
to figure out, what

00:09:17.300 --> 00:09:20.640
should we do with the
reliable persistent queue?

00:09:20.640 --> 00:09:23.510
Building one is
not an easy task.

00:09:23.510 --> 00:09:26.780
Building one that needs to keep
up with the Spotify volumes

00:09:26.780 --> 00:09:29.110
is even harder.

00:09:29.110 --> 00:09:32.490
So we wanted to use some of
the available technologies

00:09:32.490 --> 00:09:37.510
out there and use them as a
reliable persistent queue.

00:09:37.510 --> 00:09:41.010
Our adventure
started with Kafka.

00:09:41.010 --> 00:09:46.120
There were three main reasons
why we decided to experiment

00:09:46.120 --> 00:09:48.520
with Kafka.

00:09:48.520 --> 00:09:51.920
Kafka is a proven
technology that's

00:09:51.920 --> 00:09:55.310
been used all around the globe.

00:09:55.310 --> 00:09:57.770
There are many companies
being successful

00:09:57.770 --> 00:10:01.680
at using it right now.

00:10:01.680 --> 00:10:03.900
Kafka is an open-source
project which

00:10:03.900 --> 00:10:07.200
has a strong community
built around it.

00:10:07.200 --> 00:10:10.090
It is a first-class
citizen in many

00:10:10.090 --> 00:10:11.690
of the open-source
projects we were

00:10:11.690 --> 00:10:16.490
using today in Spotify--
Spark, Flink, Storm,

00:10:16.490 --> 00:10:19.660
just to name a few.

00:10:19.660 --> 00:10:23.250
Lastly, Kafka 0.8 was
designed to be used

00:10:23.250 --> 00:10:25.860
as a reliable persistent queue.

00:10:25.860 --> 00:10:27.580
This is a huge
improvement compared

00:10:27.580 --> 00:10:31.435
to Kafka 0.7, which we are using
in our current event delivery

00:10:31.435 --> 00:10:31.935
system.

00:10:34.450 --> 00:10:39.190
So we went through the docs,
followed all the guidelines

00:10:39.190 --> 00:10:42.450
and practices, and we
put a system in place.

00:10:42.450 --> 00:10:44.115
It was relatively
simple and painless.

00:10:47.602 --> 00:10:52.210
But is this how we solved
our event delivery system?

00:10:52.210 --> 00:10:56.220
Well, unfortunately not.

00:10:56.220 --> 00:11:00.870
As soon as we pushing production
volumes through this system,

00:11:00.870 --> 00:11:03.140
it started breaking apart.

00:11:03.140 --> 00:11:05.070
Mirror makers would
just drop data

00:11:05.070 --> 00:11:08.440
if we had problems with
the destination cluster.

00:11:08.440 --> 00:11:10.690
Mirror makers would go
in some confused state

00:11:10.690 --> 00:11:13.650
and completely stop
mirroring the data.

00:11:13.650 --> 00:11:16.390
We had issues with the
Kafka library, which

00:11:16.390 --> 00:11:19.590
would go into an
unrecoverable broken state

00:11:19.590 --> 00:11:24.080
if we would remove or
restart one of the brokers

00:11:24.080 --> 00:11:27.170
from the cluster.

00:11:27.170 --> 00:11:31.112
This was a lot of problems,
and at this point,

00:11:31.112 --> 00:11:32.195
we were at the crossroads.

00:11:34.960 --> 00:11:37.930
Should we significantly
invest in Kafka

00:11:37.930 --> 00:11:43.940
and make it work for us, or
should we try something else?

00:11:43.940 --> 00:11:45.090
Oops.

00:11:45.090 --> 00:11:49.810
At the same time we were
experiencing problem

00:11:49.810 --> 00:11:53.150
with Kafka, other
teams in Spotify

00:11:53.150 --> 00:11:57.170
started to experiment with
Google Cloud Services.

00:11:57.170 --> 00:12:00.500
At that time, we heard
about Cloud Pub/Sub,

00:12:00.500 --> 00:12:04.460
which seemed like a good
alternative for Kafka.

00:12:04.460 --> 00:12:06.930
When we went
through the docs, we

00:12:06.930 --> 00:12:10.700
saw that Cloud Pub/Sub
can retain and deliver

00:12:10.700 --> 00:12:16.570
data for seven days, and
that it has at least once

00:12:16.570 --> 00:12:18.980
delivery semantics.

00:12:18.980 --> 00:12:20.940
With these two
features, Cloud Pub/Sub

00:12:20.940 --> 00:12:24.590
did everything we wanted.

00:12:24.590 --> 00:12:27.500
But this was not it.

00:12:27.500 --> 00:12:32.790
Cloud Pub/Sub is a
globally available service.

00:12:32.790 --> 00:12:36.560
That means that messages
published in one region

00:12:36.560 --> 00:12:39.970
can be directly consumed
from another region.

00:12:39.970 --> 00:12:41.820
This was important
for us, because that

00:12:41.820 --> 00:12:45.470
would mean that we could skip
flaky cross-Atlantic internet

00:12:45.470 --> 00:12:49.710
links when publishing
from our US data centers

00:12:49.710 --> 00:12:51.310
and consuming in EU.

00:12:53.980 --> 00:12:59.230
Also, Cloud Pub/Sub comes
with a simple REST API.

00:12:59.230 --> 00:13:02.700
We saw this as a big
benefit, because if we didn't

00:13:02.700 --> 00:13:05.970
like Google-provided
library, we can easily

00:13:05.970 --> 00:13:11.930
write our own, because HTP
is a well-known protocol.

00:13:11.930 --> 00:13:13.900
This was not the case
with the Kafka library.

00:13:13.900 --> 00:13:16.230
We were completely
stopped in our tracks

00:13:16.230 --> 00:13:19.790
with the bugs we found there.

00:13:19.790 --> 00:13:24.080
Lastly, the best thing
about Cloud Pub/Sub,

00:13:24.080 --> 00:13:25.870
if we would use
Cloud Pub/Sub, we

00:13:25.870 --> 00:13:29.960
would have no operational
responsibility for it.

00:13:29.960 --> 00:13:32.390
Whoever carried
a pager with them

00:13:32.390 --> 00:13:35.760
knows how important this is.

00:13:35.760 --> 00:13:39.600
And after having spent
so many unslept nights

00:13:39.600 --> 00:13:45.930
with our Kafka cluster, we
appreciate it even more.

00:13:45.930 --> 00:13:49.150
Asterisk here stands for
that even if we would not

00:13:49.150 --> 00:13:51.750
be operationally responsible
for Cloud Pub/Sub,

00:13:51.750 --> 00:13:54.480
we would still be operationally
responsible for the services

00:13:54.480 --> 00:13:58.600
that are directly
interacting with it.

00:13:58.600 --> 00:14:04.510
Nevertheless, we were completely
hooked on-- seriously,

00:14:04.510 --> 00:14:06.530
there was no other
alternative for us.

00:14:06.530 --> 00:14:09.640
We just wanted to
proceed with it.

00:14:09.640 --> 00:14:13.830
But then again,
we backed up a bit

00:14:13.830 --> 00:14:18.100
and, well, stopped being so
excited and started thinking,

00:14:18.100 --> 00:14:21.010
would you actually
trust your car salesman?

00:14:21.010 --> 00:14:24.030
Was this just a sales pitch?

00:14:24.030 --> 00:14:28.120
On top of that, at the time we
started experimenting with it,

00:14:28.120 --> 00:14:31.400
Cloud Pub/Sub just
exited a beta,

00:14:31.400 --> 00:14:34.660
and there was no reports
that any other company is

00:14:34.660 --> 00:14:37.050
being successful at using it.

00:14:37.050 --> 00:14:41.340
So if we wanted to proceed
with Cloud Pub/Sub,

00:14:41.340 --> 00:14:45.550
only thing that we
needed to do was

00:14:45.550 --> 00:14:51.480
to verify that Cloud Pub/Sub
is going to work for us.

00:15:04.620 --> 00:15:07.475
So considering the
Spotify's data growth,

00:15:07.475 --> 00:15:10.070
we were mostly
interested in seeing

00:15:10.070 --> 00:15:11.885
how good Pub/Sub is at scaling.

00:15:15.130 --> 00:15:19.700
At the time, we performed both
publisher and subscriber side

00:15:19.700 --> 00:15:21.110
tests.

00:15:21.110 --> 00:15:23.340
But to make this
presentation even

00:15:23.340 --> 00:15:28.740
more interesting and exciting,
I decided to do the live demo.

00:15:28.740 --> 00:15:33.500
For the demo, I'm going to push
2 million events per second

00:15:33.500 --> 00:15:37.280
and then consume them.

00:15:37.280 --> 00:15:39.670
This number is not
just a random number.

00:15:39.670 --> 00:15:43.610
It is actually based on our
current event delivery volumes.

00:15:43.610 --> 00:15:46.920
Currently, we're delivering
around 700,000 events

00:15:46.920 --> 00:15:51.420
per second, and on top of that,
we added our growth margins

00:15:51.420 --> 00:15:54.310
and disaster recovery margins.

00:15:54.310 --> 00:15:59.320
For the demo, I'm going to use
GRPC, which is still in alpha,

00:15:59.320 --> 00:16:01.860
but it looks really promising.

00:16:01.860 --> 00:16:05.400
It is a huge
improvement compared

00:16:05.400 --> 00:16:07.990
to the autogenerated
pub/sub client,

00:16:07.990 --> 00:16:10.920
and it is also a performance
improvement compared

00:16:10.920 --> 00:16:15.660
to a sync pub/sub client that
we wrote to maximize performance

00:16:15.660 --> 00:16:20.600
over HTP and which we are
currently using in production.

00:16:20.600 --> 00:16:25.670
Disclaimer here is that even
if I'm doing the demo here

00:16:25.670 --> 00:16:27.300
with GRPC, we are
still not using

00:16:27.300 --> 00:16:30.320
GRPC in production because
we are playing it safe

00:16:30.320 --> 00:16:34.260
and we are waiting for
it to go-- to become GA.

00:16:34.260 --> 00:16:36.025
But now it's demo time.

00:16:36.025 --> 00:16:37.090
Can I get the demo?

00:16:41.450 --> 00:16:43.170
Can I?

00:16:43.170 --> 00:16:44.450
Yay, OK.

00:16:44.450 --> 00:16:52.800
So for the demo--
for the demo, I'm

00:16:52.800 --> 00:16:55.750
going to use 10 nodes to
push the million requests

00:16:55.750 --> 00:16:58.180
and then 20 nodes
to consume them.

00:16:58.180 --> 00:17:01.480
So that means that
every node is pushing

00:17:01.480 --> 00:17:04.630
200,000 events per
second, and that we're

00:17:04.630 --> 00:17:11.099
consuming from every node with
100,000 events per second.

00:17:11.099 --> 00:17:14.319
The graph should show
up here, but because it

00:17:14.319 --> 00:17:17.780
needs some warm-up time,
you can't see anything here.

00:17:17.780 --> 00:17:20.990
But I came prepared
here, unsurprisingly,

00:17:20.990 --> 00:17:24.480
and I started running the
demo before I went on stage.

00:17:24.480 --> 00:17:27.619
It is exactly the same
code and is the same demo.

00:17:27.619 --> 00:17:33.790
So you can see from 0 to 2
million without any issues.

00:17:33.790 --> 00:17:34.560
It just scaled.

00:17:34.560 --> 00:17:36.120
It just worked.

00:17:36.120 --> 00:17:38.870
The same thing is on
the subscribing side.

00:17:38.870 --> 00:17:42.120
So here we can notice it
was a bit slower to start,

00:17:42.120 --> 00:17:47.170
but as soon as picked up, it
consumed the whole backlog

00:17:47.170 --> 00:17:52.030
and then started consuming
with a steady pace.

00:17:52.030 --> 00:17:54.320
These were exactly
the same results

00:17:54.320 --> 00:17:58.080
that we observed
when we did our test.

00:17:58.080 --> 00:18:00.200
Now you can switch to
the slides, please.

00:18:03.305 --> 00:18:03.930
Slides, please.

00:18:08.190 --> 00:18:09.200
Thanks.

00:18:09.200 --> 00:18:11.650
These were exactly
the same results

00:18:11.650 --> 00:18:14.870
that we observed when
we run our initial test.

00:18:14.870 --> 00:18:19.260
So based on those results,
what was our verdict?

00:18:19.260 --> 00:18:24.930
Did we went with Pub/Sub or not?

00:18:24.930 --> 00:18:29.350
Unsurprisingly, we decided
to proceed with Cloud Pub/Sub

00:18:29.350 --> 00:18:34.120
and to base our event
delivery on top of it.

00:18:34.120 --> 00:18:37.360
After having putting it
through many hard labors,

00:18:37.360 --> 00:18:43.450
we were certain that Cloud
Pub/Sub is going to deliver.

00:18:43.450 --> 00:18:47.360
Putting it in place
was relatively simple.

00:18:47.360 --> 00:18:50.260
We already had the event
delivery service in place

00:18:50.260 --> 00:18:52.890
from our Kafka experiments,
and the only thing

00:18:52.890 --> 00:18:56.310
that we needed to do is to
replace the Kafka library

00:18:56.310 --> 00:18:58.190
with Cloud Pub/Sub one.

00:18:58.190 --> 00:19:01.970
So in our case, you can say
that Cloud Pub/Sub was just

00:19:01.970 --> 00:19:06.040
a drop-in replacement for Kafka.

00:19:06.040 --> 00:19:09.430
After we had put the Cloud
Pub/Sub to a good use,

00:19:09.430 --> 00:19:13.805
it was time to start thinking
about writing our ETL job.

00:19:17.670 --> 00:19:22.500
For the ETL job, we decided to
experiment with Dataflow SDK.

00:19:25.070 --> 00:19:29.290
Dataflow SDK is
a framework which

00:19:29.290 --> 00:19:33.770
provides us a high-level API
for writing data pipelines.

00:19:33.770 --> 00:19:38.040
It has unified bash
and stream model.

00:19:40.890 --> 00:19:42.960
Dataflow SDK comes
with a promise

00:19:42.960 --> 00:19:46.220
that it can be run on any
number of the runners that

00:19:46.220 --> 00:19:47.610
are implemented.

00:19:47.610 --> 00:19:50.970
Currently we have runners
implemented on top of Fling,

00:19:50.970 --> 00:19:56.070
on top of Spark, and finally,
on top of Cloud dataflow.

00:19:56.070 --> 00:19:58.475
Cloud dataflow is
a managed service.

00:20:01.080 --> 00:20:05.400
It is able to execute the
pipelines written with Dataflow

00:20:05.400 --> 00:20:08.560
SDK, and it comes with a
promise that it can seamlessly

00:20:08.560 --> 00:20:10.850
scale them.

00:20:10.850 --> 00:20:14.730
Back in the days, we decided
to use Cloud Dataflow runner

00:20:14.730 --> 00:20:17.000
simply because we
didn't need to put

00:20:17.000 --> 00:20:20.550
any external resources
in place before using it,

00:20:20.550 --> 00:20:22.650
and because it was
most stable one.

00:20:25.990 --> 00:20:29.980
When you write your data
pipeline with Dataflow SDK

00:20:29.980 --> 00:20:33.890
and submit it to Cloud
Dataflow, you get a fancy graph.

00:20:33.890 --> 00:20:37.990
On this graph, you can see
how your job is structured.

00:20:37.990 --> 00:20:42.610
Our ETL job in essence
does really simple things.

00:20:42.610 --> 00:20:46.580
It consumes data from a
single pub/sub subscription

00:20:46.580 --> 00:20:49.660
and writes it in parallel
to both HDFS, which

00:20:49.660 --> 00:20:52.290
stands for Hadoop
Distributed File System,

00:20:52.290 --> 00:20:56.590
and GCS, which stands
for Google Cloud Storage.

00:20:56.590 --> 00:20:59.020
Our requirement
is that all events

00:20:59.020 --> 00:21:01.550
needed to be written to Hadoop.

00:21:01.550 --> 00:21:05.550
But since we are planning
to migrate to Google Cloud,

00:21:05.550 --> 00:21:08.800
we are in parallel
writing to cloud storage.

00:21:08.800 --> 00:21:12.820
By doing this, we're enabling
other developers in Spotify

00:21:12.820 --> 00:21:16.500
to start experimenting
with Dataproc

00:21:16.500 --> 00:21:19.740
on moving their pipelines
from on-premise Hadoop

00:21:19.740 --> 00:21:20.665
into the cloud.

00:21:24.330 --> 00:21:27.400
All the events that
are being written

00:21:27.400 --> 00:21:30.200
to Hadoop and cloud
storage are being

00:21:30.200 --> 00:21:32.740
written in hourly buckets.

00:21:32.740 --> 00:21:37.010
This is the legacy from
our first event delivery

00:21:37.010 --> 00:21:42.730
system, which just copied hourly
log files from all the service

00:21:42.730 --> 00:21:47.350
machines and put them in Hadoop.

00:21:47.350 --> 00:21:51.480
This is the API that all
Spotify jobs expect today,

00:21:51.480 --> 00:21:53.550
and that means that
we're going to have

00:21:53.550 --> 00:21:56.470
this API for a long time.

00:21:56.470 --> 00:21:59.100
Difference here
with our new ETL job

00:21:59.100 --> 00:22:01.420
is that we wanted to
start incrementally

00:22:01.420 --> 00:22:05.250
filling the data as it arrives.

00:22:05.250 --> 00:22:09.420
By doing this, we wanted
to achieve lower latency

00:22:09.420 --> 00:22:10.675
for delivering data.

00:22:14.670 --> 00:22:19.330
Before the data can be consumed,
buckets, the green ones

00:22:19.330 --> 00:22:23.990
on this slide,
need to be closed.

00:22:23.990 --> 00:22:27.790
When they're closed, that means
there are safe to be consumed.

00:22:27.790 --> 00:22:31.370
In dataflow, we have
a notional watermark,

00:22:31.370 --> 00:22:34.320
which you can see as a
yellow, I don't know,

00:22:34.320 --> 00:22:40.800
thing here that's moving, which
allows us to do that easily.

00:22:40.800 --> 00:22:44.580
When the watermark passes
end of the hour boundary,

00:22:44.580 --> 00:22:48.940
we mark the bucket as closed.

00:22:48.940 --> 00:22:52.300
Once the bucket is
closed, we don't

00:22:52.300 --> 00:22:54.990
want to backfill
any late data to it.

00:22:54.990 --> 00:22:58.250
This is because most
of the Spotify jobs

00:22:58.250 --> 00:23:03.180
are reading data from an
hourly bucket only once.

00:23:03.180 --> 00:23:06.680
If we would do the backfill
in the already closed bucket,

00:23:06.680 --> 00:23:10.550
we could consider
this data as lost.

00:23:10.550 --> 00:23:15.120
So in our case, we
want to write late data

00:23:15.120 --> 00:23:17.980
in a youngest
currently open bucket.

00:23:17.980 --> 00:23:22.660
By doing this, we are
skewing the event time stamp,

00:23:22.660 --> 00:23:24.540
but we have no data loss.

00:23:27.930 --> 00:23:32.230
This is all really nice, but
how did we implement event

00:23:32.230 --> 00:23:35.680
time-based hourly buckets,
incremental bucket fill,

00:23:35.680 --> 00:23:40.220
bucket completeness,
and late data handling?

00:23:40.220 --> 00:23:43.800
Amazingly, they mostly
ended up being implemented

00:23:43.800 --> 00:23:45.956
in a single transformation.

00:23:45.956 --> 00:23:49.290
In this picture, this is
a window transformation,

00:23:49.290 --> 00:23:51.060
and what does this
transformation do,

00:23:51.060 --> 00:23:53.530
is windowing.

00:23:53.530 --> 00:23:57.560
Windowing is one of the most
powerful concepts of data flow,

00:23:57.560 --> 00:24:02.260
and I have to say one of
the most magical ones.

00:24:02.260 --> 00:24:04.710
All this stuff, bulk
of the implementation

00:24:04.710 --> 00:24:06.830
for all the concepts
I've been talking about,

00:24:06.830 --> 00:24:10.440
ended up being implemented
in only distance of lines.

00:24:10.440 --> 00:24:14.670
So in distance of lines, we
define, how big is our bucket?

00:24:14.670 --> 00:24:16.430
How do we close the window?

00:24:16.430 --> 00:24:18.170
How do we do
incremental bucket fill?

00:24:18.170 --> 00:24:22.070
And then let lastly, how
do we handle late data?

00:24:22.070 --> 00:24:24.400
Skewing of the time stamp
is not happening here,

00:24:24.400 --> 00:24:27.990
it's happening in the
transformation after,

00:24:27.990 --> 00:24:30.216
but nevertheless, the bulk
of the job is done here.

00:24:37.150 --> 00:24:43.850
Windowing is available both
in streaming and batch,

00:24:43.850 --> 00:24:47.660
but only when using streaming,
the full power of windowing

00:24:47.660 --> 00:24:49.120
is unleashed.

00:24:49.120 --> 00:24:52.510
This is because we
have a formed watermark

00:24:52.510 --> 00:24:57.870
which is being constantly
propagated as the data arrives.

00:24:57.870 --> 00:25:05.900
This allows us to detect
and handle late data.

00:25:05.900 --> 00:25:08.930
Having our ETL job being
implemented as a stream job

00:25:08.930 --> 00:25:12.070
makes perfect sense, because
event delivery is just

00:25:12.070 --> 00:25:15.050
a constant stream of data.

00:25:15.050 --> 00:25:17.130
Us having our current
event delivery job

00:25:17.130 --> 00:25:21.290
implemented as a batch job
is driven by the limitations

00:25:21.290 --> 00:25:23.310
of the framework we were using.

00:25:26.584 --> 00:25:29.470
The ETL job we've
been talking about

00:25:29.470 --> 00:25:31.610
is just in a prototype phase.

00:25:31.610 --> 00:25:33.650
We still have few
hurdles that we

00:25:33.650 --> 00:25:38.420
need to overcome for
it to go in production.

00:25:38.420 --> 00:25:42.760
First, we need to be
able to restart our ETL

00:25:42.760 --> 00:25:46.170
jobs without losing
any in-flight data.

00:25:46.170 --> 00:25:48.280
Today, this is
currently not the case

00:25:48.280 --> 00:25:51.720
if the update does not work.

00:25:51.720 --> 00:25:53.980
Other big thing is
that we need to have

00:25:53.980 --> 00:25:57.120
a good continuous
integration and deployment

00:25:57.120 --> 00:26:03.600
process for enabling the safe
and fast iterations on our ETL

00:26:03.600 --> 00:26:04.420
job.

00:26:04.420 --> 00:26:06.090
This is even more
important if you

00:26:06.090 --> 00:26:11.570
consider that we would like
to have one ETL per event type

00:26:11.570 --> 00:26:18.030
and that we are currently having
1,000 event types in Spotify.

00:26:18.030 --> 00:26:23.720
Nevertheless, preliminary
results look promising there.

00:26:23.720 --> 00:26:27.390
So on this graph, you can
see the end-to-end latency

00:26:27.390 --> 00:26:31.390
from when the event was
persisted into the log file

00:26:31.390 --> 00:26:33.835
and when it was actually
written to Hadoop.

00:26:37.160 --> 00:26:39.680
There is multiple lines here
because we are currently

00:26:39.680 --> 00:26:45.030
running four ETL jobs
in our experiments.

00:26:45.030 --> 00:26:48.380
So during the biggest
spikes, the latency

00:26:48.380 --> 00:26:52.100
goes as high as 25 minutes,
but in most of the cases

00:26:52.100 --> 00:26:55.740
is below 10 minutes.

00:26:55.740 --> 00:26:58.580
Biggest spikes here can
be attributed to the fact

00:26:58.580 --> 00:27:02.040
that we are writing to
Hadoop via flaky VPN links,

00:27:02.040 --> 00:27:05.900
and the lower spikes can
be attributed to the fact

00:27:05.900 --> 00:27:10.480
if we are still tailing files,
which is inherently unstable.

00:27:10.480 --> 00:27:14.090
They are not actually related
to the way we structured

00:27:14.090 --> 00:27:17.540
and run our data flow job.

00:27:17.540 --> 00:27:20.680
To put this in perspective,
end-to-end latency

00:27:20.680 --> 00:27:25.620
for our current event delivery
system is around two hours.

00:27:25.620 --> 00:27:30.580
So that would mean that
with our experiments,

00:27:30.580 --> 00:27:35.140
we have actually four times
the latency improvements.

00:27:38.240 --> 00:27:43.360
Delivering data is just a
first part of the process.

00:27:43.360 --> 00:27:46.760
Once the data is delivered,
other teams in Spotify

00:27:46.760 --> 00:27:51.590
are using that data to produce
really awesome recommendations

00:27:51.590 --> 00:27:53.610
to our users.

00:27:53.610 --> 00:27:57.690
Neville here is going to talk
a bit more about the tooling

00:27:57.690 --> 00:27:59.580
that they're using to do that.

00:28:03.482 --> 00:28:04.860
Here you go.

00:28:04.860 --> 00:28:07.500
NEVILLE LI: All
right, thanks Igor.

00:28:07.500 --> 00:28:09.460
So next, I'm going
to talk about Scio.

00:28:09.460 --> 00:28:11.890
It's a Scala library
that we built on top

00:28:11.890 --> 00:28:15.010
of Google Cloud Dataflow SDK.

00:28:15.010 --> 00:28:17.510
So a little bit origin story.

00:28:17.510 --> 00:28:19.590
We are big fans of
Scala at Spotify,

00:28:19.590 --> 00:28:22.290
and I work with the music
recommendation team at Spotify.

00:28:22.290 --> 00:28:25.190
That's that team
that built-- discover

00:28:25.190 --> 00:28:29.240
weekly related artists and a
few other music recommendation

00:28:29.240 --> 00:28:29.930
features.

00:28:29.930 --> 00:28:34.740
We also use-- we've been using
Scalding for three years now,

00:28:34.740 --> 00:28:37.270
and some teams are using
Spark as well for machine

00:28:37.270 --> 00:28:40.310
learning use cases.

00:28:40.310 --> 00:28:43.060
Right now, we have
about 50 users

00:28:43.060 --> 00:28:46.980
using Scala for daily
data pipeline processing.

00:28:46.980 --> 00:28:49.440
A lot of them have no
computer science background.

00:28:49.440 --> 00:28:52.720
They're from business
or math backgrounds,

00:28:52.720 --> 00:28:54.970
so not great at
programming, but they

00:28:54.970 --> 00:28:58.340
enjoy using Scala for
the concise syntax

00:28:58.340 --> 00:29:00.290
and the libraries.

00:29:00.290 --> 00:29:06.000
And we have 400-plus unique
jobs at Spotify running daily.

00:29:06.000 --> 00:29:08.887
So early 2015, about
a year and a half

00:29:08.887 --> 00:29:12.820
ago, when we started working
on GCP, Google Cloud,

00:29:12.820 --> 00:29:14.970
I started hacking
around dataflow

00:29:14.970 --> 00:29:20.120
and built a prototype
Scala APR on top of that.

00:29:20.120 --> 00:29:22.640
So compared to the
other options we have,

00:29:22.640 --> 00:29:24.210
what are the pros and cons?

00:29:24.210 --> 00:29:25.880
So today we have Scalding.

00:29:25.880 --> 00:29:26.820
It is very popular.

00:29:26.820 --> 00:29:29.940
It's open source by Twitter and
used by a bunch of other tech

00:29:29.940 --> 00:29:30.980
companies.

00:29:30.980 --> 00:29:33.990
It runs on Hadoop, which is
very stable, mature, and proven.

00:29:33.990 --> 00:29:39.290
It scales to-- it has
really high scalability.

00:29:39.290 --> 00:29:41.905
And the downside, of
course, is the operation

00:29:41.905 --> 00:29:43.390
of the Hadoop cluster.

00:29:43.390 --> 00:29:46.500
Today we have a
2,300-node Hadoop cluster,

00:29:46.500 --> 00:29:49.160
and it requires a
lot of maintenance.

00:29:49.160 --> 00:29:51.070
It doesn't handle
multi-tenancy very well,

00:29:51.070 --> 00:29:55.000
so the Hadoop
cluster is overloaded

00:29:55.000 --> 00:29:58.400
during weekdays but
underutilized on the weekend.

00:29:58.400 --> 00:30:01.780
And of course, there's no
streaming mode in Hadoop.

00:30:01.780 --> 00:30:04.500
Some of you might have
a heard of Summingbird,

00:30:04.500 --> 00:30:06.290
which is a Twitter
library that runs

00:30:06.290 --> 00:30:10.332
both on Scalding as batch mode
and Storm as a streaming mode.

00:30:10.332 --> 00:30:11.790
But the downside,
of course, is you

00:30:11.790 --> 00:30:14.290
have to maintain another
storm cluster, which adds

00:30:14.290 --> 00:30:18.870
on additional operational cost.

00:30:18.870 --> 00:30:22.610
So Spark is really getting
really popular these days.

00:30:22.610 --> 00:30:25.990
It works on both batch
and streaming mode.

00:30:25.990 --> 00:30:28.450
On top of that, it
has interactive repo,

00:30:28.450 --> 00:30:30.240
and also supports SQL.

00:30:30.240 --> 00:30:35.070
It has a very large library of
ML, machine learning algorithms

00:30:35.070 --> 00:30:36.150
and graph algorithms.

00:30:36.150 --> 00:30:40.460
It also supports Scala,
Java, Python, and even R,

00:30:40.460 --> 00:30:43.460
so it's very popular
among data scientists.

00:30:43.460 --> 00:30:47.040
The downside of Spark, of
course, and many people

00:30:47.040 --> 00:30:50.420
complain it has too
many parameters, a lot

00:30:50.420 --> 00:30:53.470
of different operational modes.

00:30:53.470 --> 00:30:55.470
The common practice of
running Spark on cloud

00:30:55.470 --> 00:30:58.360
is to set up one
cluster per job.

00:30:58.360 --> 00:31:03.720
It sounds easy, but to manage
that many Spark clusters is not

00:31:03.720 --> 00:31:07.270
an easy task, and
there isn't as much

00:31:07.270 --> 00:31:10.100
automation or tooling for that.

00:31:10.100 --> 00:31:13.310
So that's another consideration.

00:31:13.310 --> 00:31:16.180
So why do we want to
use dataflow with Scala?

00:31:16.180 --> 00:31:18.550
We already talked
about dataflow.

00:31:18.550 --> 00:31:22.050
Cloud dataflow has
a hosted solution,

00:31:22.050 --> 00:31:23.790
so it runs on Google
infrastructure

00:31:23.790 --> 00:31:25.710
and there's no operational cost.

00:31:25.710 --> 00:31:27.840
You submit your
job from a laptop.

00:31:27.840 --> 00:31:30.510
It sends some
request to the cloud.

00:31:30.510 --> 00:31:33.970
[INAUDIBLE] VMs does all
the scaling under the hood.

00:31:33.970 --> 00:31:36.230
There's no operation
cost whatsoever.

00:31:36.230 --> 00:31:40.000
It also has a tight integration
with the entire Google

00:31:40.000 --> 00:31:41.890
ecosystem, so you
can read and write

00:31:41.890 --> 00:31:45.490
from all these awesome data
products, like BigQuery

00:31:45.490 --> 00:31:48.770
and Bigtable, so
that's very nice.

00:31:48.770 --> 00:31:51.100
And the cloud dataflow
has a very simple API

00:31:51.100 --> 00:31:56.650
that handles both streaming and
batch in the same abstract data

00:31:56.650 --> 00:31:59.110
model, which is also
very nice, so you

00:31:59.110 --> 00:32:02.440
can write your application
once and run in either modes.

00:32:02.440 --> 00:32:04.140
And we also like
Scala, of course,

00:32:04.140 --> 00:32:08.240
because it's easy to build
a very high-level DSL on top

00:32:08.240 --> 00:32:10.030
of Java libraries.

00:32:10.030 --> 00:32:12.940
It's very easy for
developers to pick up.

00:32:12.940 --> 00:32:15.290
And also, Scala being a
functional programming

00:32:15.290 --> 00:32:19.390
language, is encourages the
reusable composable patterns--

00:32:19.390 --> 00:32:22.090
makes code easy to
troubleshoot, easy to maintain

00:32:22.090 --> 00:32:23.190
in the long run.

00:32:23.190 --> 00:32:25.040
We also use a lot
of Scala libraries,

00:32:25.040 --> 00:32:29.880
like Breeze, which is a
statistical numerical library,

00:32:29.880 --> 00:32:32.250
similar to NumPy,
and also [INAUDIBLE].

00:32:32.250 --> 00:32:36.440
It's a Twitter library for
approximate algorithms and also

00:32:36.440 --> 00:32:39.570
parallel processing.

00:32:39.570 --> 00:32:42.490
So this is how it
looks like, the Scio

00:32:42.490 --> 00:32:44.780
library on top of dataflow SDK.

00:32:44.780 --> 00:32:47.340
So on the top layer, you
have this very same layer

00:32:47.340 --> 00:32:50.340
of Scala API that wraps
around on the Java SDK.

00:32:50.340 --> 00:32:53.370
We also added a
few other things,

00:32:53.370 --> 00:32:58.290
like enabling the user to use
Scala libraries like Breeze

00:32:58.290 --> 00:32:59.250
and Algebird.

00:32:59.250 --> 00:33:02.400
We added interactive
repo in addition

00:33:02.400 --> 00:33:04.370
to the batch and streaming mode.

00:33:04.370 --> 00:33:06.320
And also we added
some extra features

00:33:06.320 --> 00:33:09.670
that we like, for example,
reading and writing HDFX,

00:33:09.670 --> 00:33:12.870
and also some simple
primitive for doing job

00:33:12.870 --> 00:33:15.790
dependence and scheduling.

00:33:15.790 --> 00:33:17.920
All right, so what
exactly is Scio?

00:33:17.920 --> 00:33:19.640
It's pronounced
shee-oh, obviously.

00:33:19.640 --> 00:33:22.300
Thanks to Google
Translate, it's Latin,

00:33:22.300 --> 00:33:25.390
the kind that the pope
speaks, so very classy.

00:33:25.390 --> 00:33:28.200
And also, it's the name
of a Scala data library

00:33:28.200 --> 00:33:30.400
that starts with
SC, ends with IO,

00:33:30.400 --> 00:33:32.480
so totally buzzword compliant.

00:33:32.480 --> 00:33:34.150
So we like the name.

00:33:34.150 --> 00:33:37.250
And the core API is actually
very similar to Spark--

00:33:37.250 --> 00:33:41.350
I would say about
80% compatible.

00:33:41.350 --> 00:33:43.880
And also, we borrowed
some really nice ideas

00:33:43.880 --> 00:33:47.720
from Scalding as well, the two
libraries that we really like.

00:33:47.720 --> 00:33:51.650
So it is open source on GitHub.

00:33:51.650 --> 00:33:54.560
And next, of course,
this is a data library,

00:33:54.560 --> 00:33:56.850
so I have to show the
word count example.

00:33:56.850 --> 00:33:59.370
For those of you who have
experience with Spark,

00:33:59.370 --> 00:34:02.270
the source code looks
almost exactly the same.

00:34:02.270 --> 00:34:07.700
So you read a text file, spit
out lines, do some filtering,

00:34:07.700 --> 00:34:09.889
count by value, save it
to another text file,

00:34:09.889 --> 00:34:13.290
and that's it--
five lines of code.

00:34:13.290 --> 00:34:15.000
Of course, this is
a Google conference,

00:34:15.000 --> 00:34:18.590
so I have to mention page rank
as well, in 13 lines of code.

00:34:18.590 --> 00:34:23.060
So your input is a collection
of source and destination URIs,

00:34:23.060 --> 00:34:28.010
and the initialized page rank
to 1.0 over 10 iterations.

00:34:28.010 --> 00:34:32.230
You distributed the page rank
to the destination pages,

00:34:32.230 --> 00:34:35.719
and also added on top have
some dampening factor,

00:34:35.719 --> 00:34:36.520
and that's it.

00:34:36.520 --> 00:34:40.469
That's page ranking--
13 lines of code.

00:34:40.469 --> 00:34:43.679
Very easy to write, very
easy to refactor and debug.

00:34:46.830 --> 00:34:49.590
There was a few talks
on BigQuery already,

00:34:49.590 --> 00:34:50.860
or some BigQueries.

00:34:50.860 --> 00:34:53.110
And of course we like
BigQuery as well.

00:34:53.110 --> 00:34:58.810
And we even want to use
BigQuery as much as possible.

00:34:58.810 --> 00:35:02.320
We actually at Spotify have
been through a few different SQL

00:35:02.320 --> 00:35:04.350
on Hadoop solutions.

00:35:04.350 --> 00:35:07.320
Of course, at the very
beginning there is Hive,

00:35:07.320 --> 00:35:11.050
and we use Hive on top
of TSV or Avro data.

00:35:11.050 --> 00:35:12.660
And the obvious
problem with that

00:35:12.660 --> 00:35:16.610
is that TSV or Avro, they are
all row-based storage, which

00:35:16.610 --> 00:35:18.800
means any simple SQL
query will require

00:35:18.800 --> 00:35:21.160
scanning through
the entire data set,

00:35:21.160 --> 00:35:23.250
and that's super inefficient.

00:35:23.250 --> 00:35:27.190
And also, there's no integration
with other frameworks,

00:35:27.190 --> 00:35:30.520
so it's as easy to use SQL
inside a data pipeline,

00:35:30.520 --> 00:35:33.150
for example.

00:35:33.150 --> 00:35:35.370
We've also been
experimenting with Parquet,

00:35:35.370 --> 00:35:36.820
which is a columnal storage.

00:35:36.820 --> 00:35:39.550
It is actually
inspired by Dremel,

00:35:39.550 --> 00:35:43.840
this Google paper which is
basically the technical basis

00:35:43.840 --> 00:35:45.320
of BigQuery.

00:35:45.320 --> 00:35:51.560
So Parquet gave us like 10 times
speedup over regular Avro Hive.

00:35:51.560 --> 00:35:54.150
But there are also
some obvious problems.

00:35:54.150 --> 00:35:56.970
The integration with Hive
is a little bit immature.

00:35:56.970 --> 00:35:58.700
There are some rough edges.

00:35:58.700 --> 00:36:00.550
It works really
well in Spark SQL,

00:36:00.550 --> 00:36:04.740
but the problem is Spark
is another platform that we

00:36:04.740 --> 00:36:08.880
have to tune, and it's
slightly harder to scale up.

00:36:08.880 --> 00:36:10.580
Of course, we want
to use Parquet

00:36:10.580 --> 00:36:14.900
inside the current frameworks
that we have, like Scalding,

00:36:14.900 --> 00:36:19.270
but there are some impedance
mismatch with, for example,

00:36:19.270 --> 00:36:19.770
Avro.

00:36:19.770 --> 00:36:22.890
And also, the Parquet
API is Java-based

00:36:22.890 --> 00:36:24.760
and it's a little bit clunky.

00:36:24.760 --> 00:36:27.860
Basically, instead of
having to write SQL query,

00:36:27.860 --> 00:36:31.360
you can select where
and all that stuff.

00:36:31.360 --> 00:36:33.600
In Parquet, you have to
write a lot of Java code

00:36:33.600 --> 00:36:40.620
to perform these kind of column
projection and row predicate.

00:36:40.620 --> 00:36:44.100
All right, so we want to use
BigQuery with Scio, of course.

00:36:44.100 --> 00:36:47.700
The idea is that BigQuery is
so performant and also scales

00:36:47.700 --> 00:36:49.350
independently,
which means we don't

00:36:49.350 --> 00:36:51.140
have to maintain any cluster.

00:36:51.140 --> 00:36:53.780
So the idea is to
use BigQuery for most

00:36:53.780 --> 00:36:55.780
of the slicing and dicing.

00:36:55.780 --> 00:36:59.210
And of course BigQuery has a
lot of commercial tools support

00:36:59.210 --> 00:37:02.270
and has the web UI,
so data scientists

00:37:02.270 --> 00:37:06.700
can easily prototype, say, a
data pipeline inside BigQuery

00:37:06.700 --> 00:37:10.917
and then import that code into
a data pipeline like Scio.

00:37:10.917 --> 00:37:12.500
And of course there
are certain things

00:37:12.500 --> 00:37:15.130
that's hard to do in
BigQuery or in SQL

00:37:15.130 --> 00:37:17.200
in general, like
custom machine learning

00:37:17.200 --> 00:37:21.750
algorithms or fancy statistical
analysis, things like that.

00:37:21.750 --> 00:37:24.350
So in that case, we can
copy/paste the SQL query

00:37:24.350 --> 00:37:27.080
into a data pipeline
and seamlessly

00:37:27.080 --> 00:37:30.910
integrate with a dataflow.

00:37:30.910 --> 00:37:33.050
And because we use
Scala and Scala

00:37:33.050 --> 00:37:37.420
gives us macro, which
generates type safe data types,

00:37:37.420 --> 00:37:40.220
and I'm going to
show it in a bit.

00:37:40.220 --> 00:37:43.540
On the left side, you
have this typical pipeline

00:37:43.540 --> 00:37:46.350
that handles BigQuery
as JSON objects.

00:37:46.350 --> 00:37:49.000
So obviously it's a map
of streamed objects,

00:37:49.000 --> 00:37:52.520
and you have to access
fields by string

00:37:52.520 --> 00:37:55.250
and then cast them into
whatever you think it is.

00:37:55.250 --> 00:37:57.960
It's a very tedious and
error-prone process.

00:37:57.960 --> 00:38:01.740
So you can easily run
into [INAUDIBLE] errors,

00:38:01.740 --> 00:38:04.980
and it takes a long time to
actually run the pipeline

00:38:04.980 --> 00:38:07.950
production and troubleshoot it.

00:38:07.950 --> 00:38:11.780
On the right-hand side, I have
a class, but no definition.

00:38:11.780 --> 00:38:14.230
It just generates
the class schema

00:38:14.230 --> 00:38:16.580
from the data type
inside BigQuery,

00:38:16.580 --> 00:38:21.430
and then I can treat my
data in a type safe manner.

00:38:21.430 --> 00:38:24.960
And in this case, if a
composite works in general.

00:38:27.540 --> 00:38:30.830
The next is an example of
a Spotify running feature.

00:38:30.830 --> 00:38:33.390
So this is when you
open up your phone app

00:38:33.390 --> 00:38:37.260
and run to a certain beat, so
Spotify will recommend tracks

00:38:37.260 --> 00:38:40.470
that you might like based on
the speed of your running.

00:38:40.470 --> 00:38:41.520
So some SQL, right?

00:38:41.520 --> 00:38:46.990
We have 60 million tracks,
say, 30 million active users.

00:38:46.990 --> 00:38:51.070
For each of the 10 tempo buckets
that you might be running at,

00:38:51.070 --> 00:38:53.600
we want to find a
certain number of tracks

00:38:53.600 --> 00:38:55.100
that might fit your taste.

00:38:55.100 --> 00:38:59.940
So we have a lot of features
like audio analysis, metadata,

00:38:59.940 --> 00:39:04.260
and also collaborative filtering
feature from latent vectors.

00:39:04.260 --> 00:39:07.510
We want to leverage all these
features to recommend tracks

00:39:07.510 --> 00:39:09.910
to users.

00:39:09.910 --> 00:39:11.450
Of course, as a
data scientist, I

00:39:11.450 --> 00:39:13.630
can start prototyping
in BigQuery.

00:39:13.630 --> 00:39:15.590
So this is a fairly
complex query.

00:39:15.590 --> 00:39:17.280
I can play around
with the criteria

00:39:17.280 --> 00:39:19.780
and just figure out
tracks that might look

00:39:19.780 --> 00:39:22.950
good for running a product.

00:39:22.950 --> 00:39:26.020
Next, I copy and paste the
feature, the SQL query,

00:39:26.020 --> 00:39:27.350
into a data pipeline.

00:39:27.350 --> 00:39:29.240
This is how the
pipeline looks like

00:39:29.240 --> 00:39:32.680
from a high-level perspective.

00:39:32.680 --> 00:39:36.170
So on the top row, I select
tracks and the features

00:39:36.170 --> 00:39:40.330
that I'd like to use from
BigQuery inside Scala code,

00:39:40.330 --> 00:39:42.250
do some group by
based on recording,

00:39:42.250 --> 00:39:45.730
basically filtering out
duplicate different versions

00:39:45.730 --> 00:39:46.730
of a track.

00:39:46.730 --> 00:39:50.700
And it also finds tops tracks
for each artist, groups them

00:39:50.700 --> 00:39:54.450
into tempos, then I build
a vector representation

00:39:54.450 --> 00:39:57.220
using the locality-sensitive
hashing algorithm

00:39:57.220 --> 00:40:00.680
to build basically an
index that is small enough

00:40:00.680 --> 00:40:01.860
to fit in memory.

00:40:01.860 --> 00:40:05.210
Then I can use a side
input to replicate that

00:40:05.210 --> 00:40:07.930
to each [INAUDIBLE],
which then selects

00:40:07.930 --> 00:40:10.700
from another table
of all the users.

00:40:10.700 --> 00:40:13.410
So I scan through
all the users using

00:40:13.410 --> 00:40:16.530
the locality-sensitive
hashing index

00:40:16.530 --> 00:40:19.610
to look up related tracks
the user might like.

00:40:19.610 --> 00:40:20.510
And then at the end.

00:40:20.510 --> 00:40:24.230
I can store the data to
either datastore, Bigtable

00:40:24.230 --> 00:40:26.480
for serving the
live traffic, or I

00:40:26.480 --> 00:40:30.980
can store it to a BigQuery
table for interactive analysis.

00:40:30.980 --> 00:40:31.800
So that's the idea.

00:40:34.350 --> 00:40:37.820
So this is how it looks
in the dataflow console.

00:40:37.820 --> 00:40:39.860
And one nice thing
we added on top

00:40:39.860 --> 00:40:46.610
is the file name, and also line
number in the transformation

00:40:46.610 --> 00:40:47.190
name.

00:40:47.190 --> 00:40:50.040
And because it's Scala, so
each transformation is usually

00:40:50.040 --> 00:40:52.130
a one-liner, so by just
looking at the code

00:40:52.130 --> 00:40:54.280
and skimming through
the code, you

00:40:54.280 --> 00:40:56.690
can easily visualize
it to a diagram here.

00:40:56.690 --> 00:41:01.050
And you can see we have two type
BigQuery inputs, a few filter

00:41:01.050 --> 00:41:06.480
and maps, typical functional
programming terminology.

00:41:06.480 --> 00:41:11.520
This is the second half of
a few further MapReduce,

00:41:11.520 --> 00:41:16.090
of course, side input, and
then save it to BigQuery.

00:41:16.090 --> 00:41:18.230
As you can see here,
the last transform here,

00:41:18.230 --> 00:41:21.940
save as BigQuery,
it's line number 152.

00:41:21.940 --> 00:41:24.571
So the entire pipeline is
less than 200 lines of code,

00:41:24.571 --> 00:41:27.120
and half of it is SQL.

00:41:27.120 --> 00:41:29.410
So it's actually
less than 100 lines

00:41:29.410 --> 00:41:33.640
of code for a pretty complex
machine learning product,

00:41:33.640 --> 00:41:36.670
and that took like
two hours to write.

00:41:36.670 --> 00:41:39.640
So it is pretty nice.

00:41:39.640 --> 00:41:42.140
[APPLAUSE]

00:41:42.140 --> 00:41:47.140
Of course, there
are a few catches.

00:41:47.140 --> 00:41:50.480
This is a very new project,
so there are some rough edges,

00:41:50.480 --> 00:41:54.810
and it's still under
heavy development.

00:41:54.810 --> 00:41:58.040
So right now there is
no interactive mode,

00:41:58.040 --> 00:41:59.720
although we are
working on a shield

00:41:59.720 --> 00:42:03.460
repo, so similar to
Spark, Scio, or iPython

00:42:03.460 --> 00:42:06.170
notebook, where you can
do interactive query

00:42:06.170 --> 00:42:07.510
and processing.

00:42:07.510 --> 00:42:08.010
Of course.

00:42:08.010 --> 00:42:11.190
Google provides Data Lab,
which integrates BigQuery

00:42:11.190 --> 00:42:14.110
with iPython or Jupiter.

00:42:14.110 --> 00:42:17.470
So that's another alternative
for doing interactive analysis

00:42:17.470 --> 00:42:18.740
on Google Cloud.

00:42:18.740 --> 00:42:21.010
Right now, there
isn't enough machine

00:42:21.010 --> 00:42:25.020
learning algorithms build for
BigQuery, but the instance

00:42:25.020 --> 00:42:28.890
of flow, also from
Google, which is now

00:42:28.890 --> 00:42:31.370
offered as Google Cloud ML.

00:42:31.370 --> 00:42:35.700
So in the future, it's going
to have dataflow integration.

00:42:35.700 --> 00:42:38.390
So potentially you can
just run your dataflow job

00:42:38.390 --> 00:42:41.040
and integrate with
instance of flow.

00:42:41.040 --> 00:42:44.320
Of course, the library is open
source under Apache 2 license,

00:42:44.320 --> 00:42:46.630
so feel free to check it out.

00:42:46.630 --> 00:42:49.830
There's even a prebuilt
binary job for the repo,

00:42:49.830 --> 00:42:56.010
and feel free to submit pool
requests or file issues.

00:42:56.010 --> 00:42:59.137
That's it, and next I'm going
to hand it back to Igor.

00:42:59.137 --> 00:43:02.059
[APPLAUSE]

00:43:05.960 --> 00:43:07.640
IGOR MARAVIC:
Thank you, Neville.

00:43:07.640 --> 00:43:09.890
So I'm going to steal
a bit of your time

00:43:09.890 --> 00:43:13.410
to talk about our learnings.

00:43:13.410 --> 00:43:17.940
First, I'm really glad that
we're put both Kafka and Cloud

00:43:17.940 --> 00:43:24.390
Pub/Sub through iterative
testing, and then at the end,

00:43:24.390 --> 00:43:28.032
that we chose the best
product for the job.

00:43:28.032 --> 00:43:29.275
Can I get the demo?

00:43:38.360 --> 00:43:42.710
So this is the demo I started
earlier doing my talk.

00:43:42.710 --> 00:43:44.510
And the nice part
about it, and this

00:43:44.510 --> 00:43:48.390
is the part I like about
Cloud Pub/Sub-- it just works.

00:43:48.390 --> 00:43:54.470
So as you can see, from 0
to 2 million in live demo

00:43:54.470 --> 00:43:56.910
without any issues.

00:43:56.910 --> 00:44:00.100
Same thing here is for
the consumption-- a slower

00:44:00.100 --> 00:44:02.650
to start, but
nevertheless, it picked up,

00:44:02.650 --> 00:44:07.410
and then 2 million events
steadily-- really nice.

00:44:07.410 --> 00:44:08.710
Can I back to the slide?

00:44:17.910 --> 00:44:22.140
Even if you're not in
production, our event delivery

00:44:22.140 --> 00:44:27.370
system, choice of Cloud
Pub/Sub is already paying off.

00:44:27.370 --> 00:44:32.510
It allows us to move faster
on the real-time use case.

00:44:32.510 --> 00:44:36.160
Currently, we use Cloud Pub/Sub
as a drop-in replacement

00:44:36.160 --> 00:44:39.290
for Kafka in few of
our storm topologies,

00:44:39.290 --> 00:44:45.360
for doing the real-time ad
targeting with great success.

00:44:45.360 --> 00:44:48.960
From the dataflow perspective,
I think windowing concepts might

00:44:48.960 --> 00:44:51.560
be a bit harder to
understand, but I

00:44:51.560 --> 00:44:55.200
think they're
worthwhile investing in.

00:44:55.200 --> 00:44:59.020
This is because they
allow developers

00:44:59.020 --> 00:45:02.550
to obstruct really hard problems
and focus on the business use

00:45:02.550 --> 00:45:04.960
case directly.

00:45:04.960 --> 00:45:09.060
All in all, I had
and I still have

00:45:09.060 --> 00:45:12.760
a lot of fun working on
moving our event delivery

00:45:12.760 --> 00:45:14.475
system into the cloud.

00:45:19.530 --> 00:45:22.750
If you're interested in
learning more about this,

00:45:22.750 --> 00:45:25.530
I would recommend you to
go on labs.spotify.com

00:45:25.530 --> 00:45:27.910
and check out the blog posts.

00:45:27.910 --> 00:45:31.150
There are three blog post there
describing the whole journey

00:45:31.150 --> 00:45:32.010
that we took.

00:45:34.550 --> 00:45:38.260
I hope you enjoyed, and I hope
you learned something new.

00:45:38.260 --> 00:45:42.030
If you have any questions, feel
free to catch me or Neville.

00:45:42.030 --> 00:45:42.630
And that's it.

00:45:42.630 --> 00:45:43.930
[APPLAUSE]

00:45:44.430 --> 00:45:46.580
[MUSIC PLAYING]

